  "worked_solution": "Finding the kernel of $\\,T\\,$ is equivalent to finding the null space of the standard matrix of $\\,T.$\n\nFor the given linear transformation, the standard matrix is $T=[\\begin{aligned}4 & -2 \\\\ -6 & 3\\end{aligned}].$ \n\nSetting $x=[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}],$ we then need to solve the equation $Tx=0.$ First, we reduce $T$ to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & -2 \\\\ -6 & 3\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+\\frac{6}{4}{R}_{1} \\\\  & \u223c[\\begin{aligned}4 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe basic variables are the variables whose coefficients are pivots. In this case, the basic variable is $x$, and we have one free variable, $y.$ That means we need to find $x$ in terms of $y.$\n\nFrom the first equation, we get \n\n$$\nx=\\frac{1}{2}y.\n$$\n\n  \n\nTherefore, if $x\\in \\text{Ker}(T),$ then \n\n$$\nx=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}y \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=y[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,y\\in R.\n$$\n\nThis implies that $B={[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}$ is a basis of $\\text{Ker}(T).$",
